the probability that Speaker generates the message \emph{m}, where
\emph{\{.\}} is the stop-gradient operation,
\emph{PS}(\emph{m\textbar θS})

\emph{b} the running-mean baseline used to reduce variance (Williams,
1992). We also promote exploration by en-couraging Speaker's entropy
(Williams and Peng, 1991).

The gradient of (4) w.r.t \emph{θL} is found via conventional
back-propagation (A) while gradient w.r.t \emph{θS} is found with a
REINFORCE-like procedure estimating the gra-dient via a Monte-Carlo
integration calculated over sam-ples of the messages (B). Once the
gradient is estimated, it is eventually passed to the Adam optimizer
(Kingma and Ba, 2014).
\end{quote}

