\textbf{ZLA significance score} \emph{pZLA}\textbf{:} Let's note
(\emph{li})\emph{i∈I} a distribution of message lengths of a code. As a
ZLA distribution is the one that minimizes \emph{Ltoken}, we can check
if (\emph{li})\emph{i∈I} follows ZLA by testing if its \emph{Ltoken} is
lower than any random permutation of its frequency-length mapping. This
is the idea of the randomization test proposed by Ferrer i Cancho et al.
(2013).

The test checks whether \emph{Ltoken} coincides with

puts. We can eventually compute a p-value \emph{pZLA} (at
\emph{i∈Ilifσ}(\emph{i}), with \emph{σ}(\emph{i}) a random permutation
of in-

threshold \emph{α}) that measures to which extent \emph{Ltoken} is
likely to be smaller than any other weighted mean mes-sage length of a
frequency-length mapping. \emph{pZLA \textless{} α}indicates that any
random permutation would have most likely longer weighted mean length.
Thus (\emph{li})\emph{i∈I} fol-lows \emph{significantly} a ZLA
distribution. Additional details are provided in Appendix A.3.2.
\end{quote}\strut
\end{minipage}} \\
