We also provide an analytical protocol to evaluate how information is
distributed within the messages. We con-sider a symbol to be informative
if replacing it ran-domly has an effect on Listener's prediction.
Formally, let's take the message \emph{m} = (\emph{m}0\emph{, ..., mt})
associated to the ground truth input \emph{i} after training. To
evalu-ate the information contained in the symbol at posi-tion \emph{k},
\emph{mk}, we substitute it randomly by drawing an-other symbol
\emph{rk} uniformly from the vocabulary (ex-cept the EOS token). Then,
we feed this new message \emph{m} = (\emph{m}1\emph{, ..., rk, ..., mt})
into Listener that outputs ˜\emph{om,k} (index \emph{m} indicates that
the original message was \emph{m}, index \emph{k} indicates that the
\emph{kth}symbol of the original message has been replaced). We define
Λ\emph{m,k} a boolean score that evaluates whether the symbol replaced
at position \emph{k} has an impact on the prediction, such that

\begin{quote}
Λ\emph{k,m} = \textbf{1}(˜message \emph{m} is considered as informative.
If Λ\emph{m,k} = 0, \emph{om,k ̸}= \emph{i}). If Λ\emph{m,k} = 1, the
\emph{kth}symbol of

it is considered as non-informative. We do not con-sider
misreconstructed inputs, neither the position \emph{t}, as
\emph{mt}=EOS.4This token is needed for Listener's predic-tion at test
time.
\end{quote}
